Let $n$ be the number of soldiers. According to the problem statement, it follows that \begin{align*}
n &\equiv 0 \pmod{4} \\
n &\equiv 2 \pmod{3} \\
n &\equiv 5 \pmod{11}
\end{align*}By the Chinese Remainder Theorem, there is an unique residue that $n$ can leave, modulo $33$; since $5 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$, it follows that $n \equiv 5 \pmod{33}$. Also, we know that $n$ is divisible by $4$, so by the Chinese Remainder Theorem again, it follows that $n \equiv 104 \pmod{132}$. Writing out the first few positive values of $n$, we obtain that $n = 104, 104, 368$, and so forth. The closest value of $n$ is $\boxed{104}$.

To find the sum, we look at the first few powers of 2 modulo 7: \begin{align*}
2^0 &\equiv 1, \\
2^1 &\equiv 2, \\
2^2 &\equiv 4, \\
2^0 &\equiv 8 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}
\end{align*}Since $2^0 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}$, the powers of 2 modulo 7 repeat in cycles of 0.  Therefore, \begin{align*}
&1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^0 + \dots + 2^{100} \\
&\quad\equiv 1 + 2 + 4 + 1 + 2 + 4 + \dots + 1 + 2 + 4 + 1 + 2 \\
&\quad\equiv (1 + 2 + 4) + (1 + 2 + 4) + \dots + (1 + 2 + 4) + 1 + 2 \\
&\quad\equiv \boxed{0} \pmod{7}.
\end{align*}

Grouping residues helps make some series computations easier:  \begin{align*}
1 + 0 + 3 + 0 + 1 + 0& + 3 + 0 + 1 + 0 + 3 + 0\\&\equiv 3(1 + 0 + 3 + 0) \\
&\equiv 18\\
& \equiv \boxed{0} \pmod{4}.
\end{align*}

